Relevant columns for the task  
• Movie.year : extract every movie’s release year (stored as text → need CAST to integer)  
• Movie.MID  : can be used to join if we needed other tables, but not required here  
Conditions that will be useful  
• Movie.year IS NOT NULL  
• CAST("year" AS INTEGER) BETWEEN start_year AND start_year + 9  
——————————————————————————————————

Below are ten exploratory‑to‑final SQL statements (each limited to 20 rows unless it is the final answer).  
Every step is annotated so you can follow the reasoning.

```sql
-- 1) Peek at the raw Movie table
SELECT *
FROM "Movie"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) List all distinct years present in the data
SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS year_int
FROM "Movie"
ORDER BY year_int
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) How many films were released in each individual year?
SELECT CAST("year" AS INTEGER)  AS year_int,
       COUNT(*)                 AS films_in_year
FROM   "Movie"
GROUP  BY year_int
ORDER  BY year_int
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Find the earliest and latest release years in the set
SELECT MIN(CAST("year" AS INTEGER)) AS earliest_year,
       MAX(CAST("year" AS INTEGER)) AS latest_year
FROM   "Movie";
```

```sql
-- 5) Quick look at decade‑level distribution (e.g., 1990‑1999, 2000‑2009 …)
SELECT (CAST("year" AS INTEGER)/10)*10      AS decade_start,
       COUNT(*)                              AS films_in_decade
FROM   "Movie"
GROUP  BY decade_start
ORDER  BY decade_start
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Verify that every year string converts cleanly to an integer
SELECT "year"
FROM   "Movie"
WHERE  CAST("year" AS INTEGER) IS NULL;
```

```sql
-- 7) Count films for one example 10‑year period (2000‑2009) to see the pattern
SELECT COUNT(*) AS films_2000_2009
FROM   "Movie"
WHERE  CAST("year" AS INTEGER) BETWEEN 2000 AND 2009;
```

```sql
-- 8) Produce counts for *all* 10‑year windows, one row per distinct start year
SELECT  a.start_year,
        COUNT(*) AS films_in_window
FROM   (SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS start_year
        FROM "Movie") AS a               -- every possible window start
JOIN   "Movie"  AS b
  ON   CAST(b."year" AS INTEGER) BETWEEN a.start_year
                                      AND a.start_year + 9
GROUP  BY a.start_year
ORDER  BY a.start_year
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Same as (8) but ranked by size so we can see which window is largest
SELECT  a.start_year,
        COUNT(*) AS films_in_window
FROM   (SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS start_year
        FROM "Movie") AS a
JOIN   "Movie" AS b
  ON   CAST(b."year" AS INTEGER) BETWEEN a.start_year
                                   AND a.start_year + 9
GROUP  BY a.start_year
ORDER  BY films_in_window DESC, a.start_year
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) FINAL ANSWER: start year of the 10‑year window with most films and its count
SELECT  a.start_year,
        COUNT(*) AS films_in_window
FROM   (SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS start_year
        FROM "Movie") AS a
JOIN   "Movie" AS b
  ON   CAST(b."year" AS INTEGER) BETWEEN a.start_year
                                   AND a.start_year + 9
GROUP  BY a.start_year
ORDER  BY films_in_window DESC, a.start_year
LIMIT 1;
```

Running query 10 will return exactly two columns:  

start_year | films_in_window  

which gives the opening year of the most‑populated consecutive ten‑year span and how many movies fall inside that window.
